## 0. Constraint reconciliation (read this first)

Two requirements pull against each other:

- "one module with `for_each`" → both envs in one config
- AC 7 "changing prod does not modify dev state" → separate state files

Resolution used here: **one `for_each` block, filtered to the active environment.** Every env lives in `var.environments`, but the root only instantiates the one selected by the workspace. You get for_each-shaped addresses (`module.env["prod"]....`) with one state file per env, and dev/prod are never in the same plan.

```hcl
locals {
  active = terraform.workspace   # "dev" | "prod"
  envs   = { for k, v in var.environments : k => v if k == local.active }
}
module "env" { for_each = local.envs ... }
```

If you'd rather have a single state holding both, drop the filter — everything below still applies, but you lose AC 7 and blast-radius isolation. I don't recommend it.

---

## 1. Layout

```
├── main.tf                 # module "env" { for_each = ... }
├── moved.tf                # migration shims (deletable after apply)
├── variables.tf
├── dev.tfvars
├── prod.tfvars
├── secrets.dev.enc.yaml    # SOPS
├── secrets.prod.enc.yaml   # SOPS
└── modules/service-env/
    ├── variables.tf
    ├── s3.tf  rds.tf  eks.tf  iam.tf  cdn.tf
    └── outputs.tf
```

---

## 2. Module interface

`modules/service-env/variables.tf`:

```hcl
variable "env"    { type = string }
variable "region" { type = string }

variable "cluster" {
  type = object({
    node_count    = number
    instance_type = string
    k8s_version   = string
  })
}

variable "db" {
  type = object({
    instance_class      = string
    multi_az            = bool
    storage_encrypted   = bool
    kms_key_arn         = optional(string)
    backup_retention    = optional(number, 7)
    deletion_protection = optional(bool, true)
  })
  validation {
    condition     = var.db.backup_retention >= 7 && var.db.deletion_protection
    error_message = "AC 2: retention >= 7 days and deletion protection required."
  }
}

variable "replication" {
  type = object({
    enabled     = bool
    dest_region = optional(string)
  })
  default = { enabled = false }
}

variable "iam" {
  type = object({
    mode          = string            # "user" (dev) | "oidc_role" (prod)
    oidc_provider = optional(string)
    oidc_subject  = optional(string)
    actions       = list(string)
  })
  validation {
    condition = var.env != "prod" || !anytrue([
      for a in var.iam.actions : can(regex("\\*", a))
    ])
    error_message = "AC 4: no wildcard IAM actions in prod."
  }
}

variable "db_connection_secret" {
  type      = string
  sensitive = true                    # injected from SOPS-decrypted tfvars
}
```

The **only** thing that differs between environments is this object. Every conditional in the module keys off it — no `count = var.env == "prod" ? 1 : 0` sprinkled around except where a resource genuinely doesn't exist (replication, OIDC role).

`modules/service-env/s3.tf` (representative; note the alias declaration, which is the piece people forget):

```hcl
terraform {
  required_providers {
    aws = {
      source                = "hashicorp/aws"
      configuration_aliases = [aws.replica]
    }
  }
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket" "assets" {
  bucket = "acme-assets-${var.env}"
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket_versioning" "assets" {
  bucket = aws_s3_bucket.assets.id
  versioning_configuration { status = "Enabled" }
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket_server_side_encryption_configuration" "assets" {
  bucket = aws_s3_bucket.assets.id
  rule {
    apply_server_side_encryption_by_default {
      sse_algorithm     = var.db.kms_key_arn != null ? "aws:kms" : "AES256"
      kms_master_key_id = var.db.kms_key_arn
    }
  }
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket_public_access_block" "assets" {
  bucket                  = aws_s3_bucket.assets.id
  block_public_acls       = true
  block_public_policy     = true
  ignore_public_acls      = true
  restrict_public_buckets = true
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket" "replica" {
  count    = var.replication.enabled ? 1 : 0
  provider = aws.replica
  bucket   = "acme-assets-${var.env}-replica"
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket_replication_configuration" "assets" {
  count      = var.replication.enabled ? 1 : 0
  depends_on = [aws_s3_bucket_versioning.assets]
  bucket     = aws_s3_bucket.assets.id
  role       = aws_iam_role.replication[0].arn
  rule {
    id     = "assets"
    status = "Enabled"
    destination {
      bucket        = aws_s3_bucket.replica[0].arn
      storage_class = "STANDARD_IA"
    }
  }
}
```

RDS / EKS follow the same shape:

```hcl
resource "aws_db_instance" "main" {
  identifier              = "app-${var.env}"
  engine                  = "postgres"
  instance_class          = var.db.instance_class
  multi_az                = var.db.multi_az
  storage_encrypted       = var.db.storage_encrypted
  kms_key_id              = var.db.kms_key_arn
  backup_retention_period = var.db.backup_retention
  deletion_protection     = var.db.deletion_protection
  publicly_accessible     = false
  # ...
}

resource "aws_eks_node_group" "main" {
  instance_types = [var.cluster.instance_type]
  scaling_config {
    desired_size = var.cluster.node_count
    min_size     = var.cluster.node_count
    max_size     = var.cluster.node_count * 2
  }
  # ...
}
```

---

## 3. Root

`main.tf`:

```hcl
provider "aws" { region = local.envs[local.active].region }

provider "aws" {
  alias  = "replica"
  region = try(local.envs[local.active].replication.dest_region, "us-west-2")
}

provider "aws" {
  alias  = "us_east_1"        # ACM for CloudFront
  region = "us-east-1"
}

module "env" {
  source   = "./modules/service-env"
  for_each = local.envs

  env                  = each.key
  region               = each.value.region
  cluster              = each.value.cluster
  db                   = each.value.db
  replication          = each.value.replication
  iam                  = each.value.iam
  db_connection_secret = var.db_connection_secret

  providers = {
    aws           = aws
    aws.replica   = aws.replica
    aws.us_east_1 = aws.us_east_1
  }
}
```

`prod.tfvars`:

```hcl
environments = {
  prod = {
    region  = "us-east-1"
    cluster = { node_count = 4, instance_type = "t3.large", k8s_version = "1.29" }
    db = {
      instance_class = "db.t3.medium", multi_az = true, storage_encrypted = true
      kms_key_arn = "arn:aws:kms:us-east-1:...:key/...", backup_retention = 30
      deletion_protection = true
    }
    replication = { enabled = true, dest_region = "us-west-2" }
    iam = {
      mode = "oidc_role"
      oidc_provider = "arn:aws:iam::...:oidc-provider/oidc.eks..."
      oidc_subject  = "system:serviceaccount:app:app-sa"
      actions = ["s3:GetObject", "s3:PutObject", "kms:Decrypt", "kms:GenerateDataKey"]
    }
  }
}
```

`dev.tfvars` mirrors it with `t3.medium`/2, `db.t3.micro`, `multi_az=false`, `replication.enabled=false`, `mode="user"`.

Secret never lands in Git plaintext:

```bash
sops -d secrets.prod.enc.yaml > /dev/shm/prod.secret.tfvars
terraform apply -var-file=prod.tfvars -var-file=/dev/shm/prod.secret.tfvars
```

(Alternative: the `carlpett/sops` provider reading the encrypted file directly — but that puts the decrypted value in state either way, so keep state encrypted with SSE-KMS + a locked bucket policy.)

---

## 4. Zero-diff state moves

`moved.tf` — write moves for **both** envs; Terraform silently ignores a `moved` whose `from` doesn't exist in the current state, so the same file works in both workspaces.

```hcl
# --- dev ---
moved { from = aws_s3_bucket.dev_assets                              to = module.env["dev"].aws_s3_bucket.assets }
moved { from = aws_s3_bucket_versioning.dev_assets                   to = module.env["dev"].aws_s3_bucket_versioning.assets }
moved { from = aws_s3_bucket_server_side_encryption_configuration.dev_assets to = module.env["dev"].aws_s3_bucket_server_side_encryption_configuration.assets }
moved { from = aws_s3_bucket_public_access_block.dev_assets          to = module.env["dev"].aws_s3_bucket_public_access_block.assets }
moved { from = aws_db_instance.dev                                   to = module.env["dev"].aws_db_instance.main }
moved { from = aws_eks_cluster.dev                                   to = module.env["dev"].aws_eks_cluster.main }
moved { from = aws_eks_node_group.dev                                to = module.env["dev"].aws_eks_node_group.main }
moved { from = aws_iam_user.dev_svc                                  to = module.env["dev"].aws_iam_user.svc[0] }

# --- prod ---
moved { from = aws_s3_bucket.prod_assets                             to = module.env["prod"].aws_s3_bucket.assets }
moved { from = aws_s3_bucket.prod_assets_replica                     to = module.env["prod"].aws_s3_bucket.replica[0] }
moved { from = aws_s3_bucket_replication_configuration.prod          to = module.env["prod"].aws_s3_bucket_replication_configuration.assets[0] }
moved { from = aws_db_instance.prod                                  to = module.env["prod"].aws_db_instance.main }
moved { from = aws_eks_node_group.prod                               to = module.env["prod"].aws_eks_node_group.main }
moved { from = aws_iam_role.prod_svc                                 to = module.env["prod"].aws_iam_role.svc[0] }
moved { from = aws_iam_role_policy.prod_svc                          to = module.env["prod"].aws_iam_role_policy.svc[0] }
```

Note the `[0]` suffixes: a `count`-gated resource inside the module needs the index in the `to` address. Omitting it is the single most common cause of a destroy/create pair sneaking into the plan.

### If dev and prod were previously separate root directories

`moved` cannot cross state files. Point the new root at the existing backend key per workspace and the moves resolve normally. If you're also consolidating backends, do it as a distinct, separate step:

```bash
terraform state pull > backup-prod-$(date +%s).tfstate   # always
terraform state mv -state-out=... aws_db_instance.prod module.env\[\"prod\"\].aws_db_instance.main
```

Prefer `moved` blocks — they're reviewable in the PR diff, replayable in CI, and don't require a human at a shell.

---

## 5. Runbook

```bash
# 0. Backups, per env
for w in dev prod; do
  terraform workspace select $w
  terraform state pull > backup-$w-$(date +%Y%m%dT%H%M%S).json
done

# 1. Dev first
terraform workspace select dev
terraform init -upgrade
terraform plan -var-file=dev.tfvars -var-file=/dev/shm/dev.secret.tfvars -out=dev.plan

# 2. Gate: must be zero-diff
terraform show -json dev.plan | jq -e '
  [.resource_changes[]
   | select((.change.actions | inside(["no-op"]) | not)
            and .change.actions != ["read"])] | length == 0
' || { echo "NOT zero-diff — do not apply"; exit 1; }

terraform show dev.plan | grep -E '^\s+# .* has moved to' | wc -l   # sanity: == moved-block count

# 3. Apply (writes only the address remapping)
terraform apply dev.plan

# 4. Confirm convergence
terraform plan -var-file=dev.tfvars -var-file=/dev/shm/dev.secret.tfvars -detailed-exitcode  # expect 0

# 5. Repeat 1–4 for prod, then delete moved.tf
```

**Zero-diff gate is the whole game.** `plan` must contain only `no-op` changes plus "has moved to" lines. Any `update in place` means an attribute drifted during refactor — fix the module, never `-target` around it.

---

## 6. Failure modes I'd watch for

| Symptom | Cause | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| Bucket destroy/create | `bucket` name interpolation changed (`acme-assets-dev` vs old literal `acme-dev-assets`) | Keep exact legacy names; add a `name_override` input rather than renaming |
| RDS forces replacement | `identifier` or `db_subnet_group_name` differs | Diff old resource attrs against module defaults before moving |
| Replication resources want re-create | `aws.replica` provider not passed → module used default region | Declare `configuration_aliases`, pass explicitly in `providers = {}` |
| `count` index errors on move | `to` address missing `[0]` | Add index |
| Tags churn everywhere | Old blocks had per-env tags, module has `default_tags` | Merge; assert with a zero-diff plan before applying |
| Prod plan shows dev resources | Forgot the `local.envs` filter | Filter is load-bearing for AC 7 |
| Secret shows as changing | SOPS re-encryption changed ciphertext but not plaintext | Ensure decrypt happens before Terraform sees it; ciphertext must not be an input |

CI enforcement worth adding: a job that runs the plan against both workspaces and fails on `-detailed-exitcode == 2` for the duration of the migration PR. That turns "zero-diff" from a promise into a check.
